WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.610
One of the most exciting aspects of the lead-up
to Deadpool 2 was the arrival of the comic

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:08.950
book superhero team X-Force, a harder-edged
and more militant take on good guy mutant

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:10.120
alliances like the X-Men.

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:14.829
"I just want to say how proud I am of this
team!

00:00:14.829 --> 00:00:16.369
You guys look amazing!

00:00:16.369 --> 00:00:20.050
Vanisher, I have no doubt you look amazing
too."

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:24.820
Fans speculated on what Terry Crews' Bedlam,
Lewis Tan's Shatterstar, and Bill Skarsgård's

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:28.199
Zeitgeist would bring to the fight in the
Deadpool sequel, and where things might all

00:00:28.199 --> 00:00:30.789
be ultimately going with the X-Force team-up
movie.

00:00:30.789 --> 00:00:35.219
The possibilities seemed endless - and then
everybody died.

00:00:35.219 --> 00:00:36.899
"Really?!"

00:00:36.899 --> 00:00:40.480
The sequence in which most of the members
of Deadpool's new team all die comically during

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:44.460
a parachute jump just minutes after being
introduced is one of the funniest things to

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:48.410
happen in all of Deadpool 2 - and a lot of
that has to do with how totally unexpected

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:49.410
it was.

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:53.290
This was no accident - it took a whole lot
of work to keep that sequence a surprise,

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:57.170
and the bewildered reactions the movie earned
from audiences everywhere is actually the

00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:00.109
entire reason the movie's creative team set
out to do it.

00:01:00.109 --> 00:01:04.909
Director David Leitch said that he was "obsessed"
with the idea of tricking audiences into thinking

00:01:04.909 --> 00:01:09.009
Deadpool 2 would feature some actual, substantial
team-up action, saying:

00:01:09.009 --> 00:01:10.009
"We all loved it.

00:01:10.009 --> 00:01:13.580
It's such a great, irreverent, Deadpool world
gag that you can't not do it."

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:15.160
Ryan Reynolds couldn't have agreed more.

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:20.950
"In order to make a great, rated-R comic book
omelette, you've got to break some eggs."

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:25.380
As revealed in the special features for Deadpool
2's home media release, the filmmakers went

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:28.460
to dramatic lengths to keep X-Force's fate
top-secret.

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:32.320
In many instances, not even the crew of the
movie knew exactly what they were shooting

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:36.549
at any given moment, with exact details being
kept on a strict need-to-know basis.

00:01:36.549 --> 00:01:41.240
According to Wernick, many of the film's sides
- or on-set script pages - came in the color

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:43.369
red, making them harder to photocopy.

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:47.409
They were also frequently full of redacted
information, and all turned in and shredded

00:01:47.409 --> 00:01:48.549
at the end of every day.

00:01:48.549 --> 00:01:50.550
"It's like some spy s---, man."

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:54.369
The actors who played the X-Force members
were unable to see much, if any information

00:01:54.369 --> 00:01:56.960
about their parts during the filmmaking process.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:01.219
They were also discouraged from sharing what
info they did learn with friends and family.

00:02:01.219 --> 00:02:02.920
Even their agents didn't know what they were
doing.

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:04.689
"David called me and said, 'You can't tell
anybody.

00:02:04.689 --> 00:02:06.420
You can't even tell your representation.'

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:08.850
They know I'm in the movie, but they don't
know what I'm doing."

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:13.580
Rob Delaney, who played the gung-ho-but-useless
X-Force member Peter, was also kept in the

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:17.440
dark about many aspects of his role, despite
the fact that he's not playing a character

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:18.940
from the comic books at all.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:22.720
"Am I getting catfished here, or…"

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:26.220
The production even worked to film scenes
for the movie's promotional materials that

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:30.570
they knew would never make it to a final cut,
all for the purpose of further luring audiences

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:32.380
in for the team-up fake-out.

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:34.960
Deadpool 2 co-writer Rhett Reese told Entertainment
Weekly:

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:38.820
"What the actors were very gracious about
doing was shooting footage they knew was not

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:42.210
actually going to be in the [movie] so we
could trick people into thinking they're in

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:43.970
the movie longer than they actually were.

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:48.020
If you watch trailers and commercials, you'll
watch Bedlam and Shatterstar out on the street

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:49.270
kicking people's ass.

00:02:49.270 --> 00:02:51.740
It was all done with the knowledge it wouldn't
be in the movie."

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:56.870
It sounds like the filmmakers took some ridiculously
complicated steps to keep the plot under wraps.

00:02:56.870 --> 00:02:58.160
But then again, that is the Deadpool way.
"Maximum effort."

